    {
      "label": "safe zone (TV overscan)",
      "note": "A television may crop the edges of the picture it is handed, and neither the browser nor Android can ask it how much: overscan is a property of the panel, not of the signal. 2.5%, WHICH IS THE REAL FIGURE AND NOT GOOGLE'S. The classic \"televisions crop about 5%\" means 5% of the PICTURE, so ~2.5% per edge; Google's TV guidance doubles that to 5% a side, which is the CRT-era worst case and dates from 2014. A modern panel fed a 1:1 HDMI signal crops nothing at all. Shipped at 5% first and rejected on sight: it pushed the chrome so far in that it read as floating rather than as cornered. FRACTIONS OF THE SURFACE, not lengths, because overscan is a percentage and an authored px is not: 48px is 2.5% of a 1920 board and 3.75% of a 1280 one. Each shell converts once and reports the pair to `ttp_display_safe_insets`, which is what insets the per-cell HUD rects — see public/display/CLAUDE.md. Applied to EVERY edge of every cell and not only to the screen's own, which is a look decision argued where it is applied (ttp_display_core.cc). tvOS does NOT read these: it reports the safe area the system gives it, which is a real 90/60 pt rather than an assumption. The steer bar is deliberately NOT inset by them. It is the one HUD element the player also has in their hand, and moving it up crowds the car in a stacked cell — TtpRendererFrame.cpp says so where it places the bar.",
      "tokens": [
        "safe-frac-x",
        "safe-frac-y"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "track map (the circuit mini-map's marks)",
      "note": "The marks a circuit's mini-map is drawn with, in the schematic's OWN viewBox units (`ttp_track_schematic_json` projects into 0 0 256 256, and `schematic.cc` writes that literal). Not lengths: a tile is drawn at whatever size its slot is, so a px width would be a second, contradictory scale. THEY ARE HERE BECAUSE THREE PLATFORMS DRAW THIS PICTURE. The projection is C++'s and the strokes are each shell's, so the widths were spelled once in the CSS below, once in Swift and once in Kotlin, with nothing that could see all three — the exact shape rule 1 exists to stop. As :root tokens they ride `design-tokens.json`, which both TV shells already read at boot.",
      "tokens": [
        "track-map-casing",
        "track-map-road",
        "track-map-start-r",
        "track-map-start-ring"
      ]
    }
  ],
